WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.670
There was this 'compliment' that I got: 'Oh,
you're pretty, for a Jew.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.390
There are a lot more anti-Semitic incidents
than you might generally think.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.590
That's Anna: cancer researcher in Munich.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.600
And Doron: Judge in Berlin.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:22.230
What most people only see when it hits the
headlines, they experience in everyday life:

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.160
discrimination, because they’re Jews.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.730
It's a current and real problem.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:33.070
It never went away
It can be subtle; it can be in your face.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:37.110
Sometimes, antisemitism can be hard to recognize...even
in yourself.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.460
But no, no, no, don't run away:
You don't have to necessarily have a malicious

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:50.270
intention to say something antisemitic.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.930
I'd describe myself as a liberal Jew

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:56.730
Here, we have a traditional-oriented community.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.231
There was one bad incident where someone drove
by the front of the synagogue on a bike or

00:01:00.231 --> 00:01:03.469
a motorbike and shouted 'Heil Hitler!'.

00:01:03.469 --> 00:01:05.840
This was during drop-off time for the kids
at daycare.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.770
At about 8:30 a.m., when a lot of parents
bring their children.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.900
And then at a party I was confronted with:
'Did Hitler forget one or something?

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:22.400
Adolf Hitler was the dictator of Nazi Germany,
responsible for the systematic murder of 6

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.040
million Jews.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.680
As a Jew, what do you do in such a situation?

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.250
It’s um...I was pretty stunned.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:37.570
I was interviewed – it was supposed to be
for a feature in a major German magazine.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:43.250
Afterward we had a coffee and we were sitting
together and then out of nowhere came the

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:49.140
question: 'But come on, let's be honest...with
Jews it’s always about the money, right?'

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.250
And you just think: Wait a minute.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.469
Did I hear that right?

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:57.149
And that's just one trope that's been doing
the rounds for centuries.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:58.840
In fact:

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.670
Antisemitic tropes are nothing new

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:08.500
They've been passed down and reappear in every
era … repackaged, seemingly shiny and new,

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:14.230
for the next generation.

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:15.900
Antisemitism comes in many different forms.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:20.120
Mirjam Wenzel, Director of Frankfurt Jewish
Museum, Germany.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.010
So, let's look at that example about wealth
and money.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:32.550
Historically, of course, Jews were excluded
from many professions in Europe.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:37.440
And among the jobs they were allowed to do
was, traditionally, dealing with coins or

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.480
exchanging money.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.090
That was the case in the early modern period,
and this led to the idea that Jews have something

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.370
to do with money.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:53.280
Times of crisis provide particularly fertile
ground for spreading antisemitic tropes and

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.510
conspiracies.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.999
An age-old and murky myth: the hidden hand.

00:02:57.999 --> 00:03:06.080
You can't see it, it's not there, but there
is an assumption that there is something sinister.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.020
Arie Dubnov, History professor, George Washington
University.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.810
We saw it even in cases like the COVID pandemic,
right?

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:18.580
These questions of who exactly benefits from
COVID?

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:26.790
And then implying that actually it's a conspiracy
of a Jewish, you know, businessmen and companies

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.700
that are producing the vaccines and so on.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.159
It’s a convenient idea that there’s a
small group of people secretly controlling

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:36.159
the world.

00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:41.930
In reality, it’s an ungrounded conspiracy
theory that’s reeeally old.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:46.980
That, of course, is absolutely the core antisemitic
narrative.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.080
And it's a Christian one.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:56.390
This “hidden hand” is a baseless antisemitic
trope dating back as far as the Middle Ages,

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:01.439
when Christianity strove to become the dominant
religion in Europe – and began to persecute

00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:03.049
Jews.

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:08.939
And that means that the conspiracy theory
that Jews are doing something in secret is

00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:14.300
always part of the mobilization of feelings,
which in turn leads to acts of violence.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:20.359
I’ve confronted this a lot and I just wonder:
what do Germans actually talk about over,

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:26.561
say, family Christmas dinner if antisemitic
conspiracy theories pop up at the table?

00:04:26.561 --> 00:04:30.430
Do you stay silent or do you say something?

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:34.530
Yes, you should say something, because:

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:40.419
Ignoring antisemitism won't make it magically
go away

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:41.889
Let's stay in Germany for a while.

00:04:41.889 --> 00:04:46.780
So, why does it seem like it's so difficult
for a lot of Germans to confront antisemitism

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:47.810
today?

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.601
Maybe because they see it as something gone
and overcome?

00:04:49.601 --> 00:04:56.240
I think a lot of people associate antisemitism
solely with the Second World War and the belief

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.479
that Hitler created antisemitism and it was
all over in 1945.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:03.669
And that's just completely ahistorical.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:06.460
So that's another thing:

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.060
Antisemitism isn't just a Nazi thing

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:15.510
But, although Jews were long subjected to
violence, expulsions, forced conversions and

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:21.370
systematic persecution, the Nazi era was undoubtedly
without parallel.

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:27.289
Starting in 1933, the Nazis used antisemitic
legislation and propaganda to create a culture

00:05:27.289 --> 00:05:29.389
of segregation and hostility.

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:35.919
All over Nazi-occupied Europe, Jews were eventually
detained in ghettos and deported to concentration

00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:41.970
or death camps, where they were subjected
to forced labor, atrocities and mass murder.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:47.330
By the time Nazi Germany surrendered, six
million Jews had been murdered.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:54.910
The minute we automatically think that the
step next of antisemitism is already the Holocaust,

00:05:54.910 --> 00:06:01.520
we find it problematic to identify the more
sinister, the more mundane, the discriminatory

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:09.139
practices, the tropes, the trafficking of
stereotypes that is not going to automatically

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:13.889
lead tomorrow, God forbid, to complete nihilism
and genocidal practices.

00:06:13.889 --> 00:06:17.860
So are you an antisemite, only if you advocate
sending Jews to the gas chambers?

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.310
And anything less is legitimate criticism
and debate on the matter?

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.330
So don't be mistaken:

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.889
The bar for antisemitism isn't set at murder

00:06:26.889 --> 00:06:32.300
It can also sound like this:
You look pretty for a Jew'.

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.060
But what does that do to a person?

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:40.320
You notice it when my grandmother gives me
matzoh crackers on Passover and I put them

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.110
in the car and she says: 'No, never leave
them lying around, hide them.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.830
I don't want my neighbor to see that we're
Jewish.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:50.949
There are a lot of Jews in my circle of friends
who aren't open about being Jewish because

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:54.110
they've had bad experiences with antisemitism.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:59.030
It's just stressful for me to pretend 24/7
and I'm open about being Jewish.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:06.060
If someone has a problem with it, they should
just sort that out with me.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:10.720
I think there are many different reactions,
because it's quite distinct, even in the same

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.720
person.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.400
There ' s the feeling of: I'll get my suitcase
out.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.740
I have to be better prepared for what's happening.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.810
Can I really permanently belong to society
here?

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.389
Am I sufficiently protected?

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:25.849
Where do move to?

00:07:25.849 --> 00:07:30.629
For some Jews that one place they can feel
safe is Israel – the world's only Jewish

00:07:30.629 --> 00:07:31.639
state.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:36.940
Since 1950, Jews from all over the world and
Gentile converts to Judaism are entitled to

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:42.690
settle in Israel and receive full Israeli
citizenship if they want to.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:48.840
For me, as a practicing Jew, it's meaningful
when I can celebrate Jewish holidays there...

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.030
And, it's a safe haven.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:57.960
And there's also the feeling that there's
a country that's a place to simply be and

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:04.220
to maybe not have the feeling of being in
a minority.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:09.270
This has historically been of great importance
to Jews.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:14.150
But walking down the street wearing my Star
of David and knowing that nothing will happen

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:20.449
is an incredible feeling as a Jew.

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:25.419
But the decades since the founding of the
state of Israel have seen a new facet of antisemitism

00:08:25.419 --> 00:08:29.250
emerge: Israel-related antisemitism.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:33.839
More often than not, political events linked
to Israel also prompt a rise in antisemitic

00:08:33.839 --> 00:08:36.849
attacks...way beyond Israel's borders.

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:42.899
Especially, when there's a flare-up in the
Israeli-Palestinian conflict.

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:48.460
As soon as there's conflict in Israel, the
atmosphere here immediately becomes tougher,

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:51.320
more uncomfortable.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:57.779
So what's always a bit irritating for me is
when I say that I'm Jewish and then people

00:08:57.779 --> 00:09:01.510
criticize me for Israel's policies or blame
me for it.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:05.459
I don't have an Israeli passport, I can't
vote there, I don't have the right to vote

00:09:05.459 --> 00:09:06.459
there.

00:09:06.459 --> 00:09:11.649
I don't have a say in the political situation
and I'm not Israeli.

00:09:11.649 --> 00:09:18.660
In fact, the vast majority of Jews around
the world are not Israeli and many have no

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:20.890
connection with the country.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:25.850
When someone asks me, 'What do you think about
what’s going on in Israel right now?'

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:31.200
And without an accusatory undertone and without:
‘What are you and your people doing there

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.519
again?', but simply: 'What do you make of
it?', then I talk and, of course, I answer

00:09:34.519 --> 00:09:35.519
them normally.

00:09:35.519 --> 00:09:38.520
But, it's not what you say, it's how you say
it.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:41.690
So let’s debunk another murky myth:

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:46.630
Jews all over the world are not responsible
for Israeli policy

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:51.920
Of course, the Israeli government can be criticized,
but where do you draw the line between antisemitism

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.550
and political debate?

00:09:54.550 --> 00:10:01.331
I would draw the line in several cases, demonstrating
not in front of the Israeli embassy, but in

00:10:01.331 --> 00:10:06.050
front of a synagogue that's already starting
to blur the line.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:13.890
There you already see one of the central problems:
In criticism of the Israeli government's policy,

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:19.519
there's very often the connotation of questioning
the right to exist.

00:10:19.519 --> 00:10:28.950
Or, automatically, you're forcing Jews – whether
they want to talk about Israel or not – when

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:33.730
they enter the room to state their position
regarding Israel.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:34.730
Are you pro or against?

00:10:34.730 --> 00:10:35.730
Where do your loyalties lie?

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.019
You have to state very clearly up front.

00:10:38.019 --> 00:10:43.360
This is also, if not borderline antisemitism,
but upfront antisemitism.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:51.330
It automatically assumes that the average
Jew, per definition, by them being Jewish,

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:59.120
are in a way 'agents of the Israeli government'
and so on and so and so forth.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:05.639
So how to handle this political hot potato
– not talk about it at all?

00:11:05.639 --> 00:11:10.589
That's why I'm a bit cautious about this idea
that 'you mustn't talk about Israel at all.'

00:11:10.589 --> 00:11:15.680
Of course Israel is close to us and, of course,
it means more to most of us than many other

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:16.680
countries.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.029
But, yes, you can just talk about it normally.

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:24.750
So
Criticizing Israeli policy: fine.

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:30.700
Conflating Israeli state policy and Jewish
life: not fine, and often antisemitic.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:36.430
Today, antisemitism is even easier to spread
-- thanks to social media.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:40.380
Which means you should be extra cautious when
it comes to where you're getting your information:

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:45.410
It's like reading the fine print on any food
that we put into our body.

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:46.820
We need to be careful.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:51.020
The fact [this 'information'] is simply out
there and people are retweeting it or sharing

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:55.510
it on Facebook doesn't guarantee anything
in terms of its validity.

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:58.410
Often, unfortunately, the opposite.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:11:59.740
So with that in mind:

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:04.070
Don't believe everything you read

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:07.640
The world over, Jews experience antisemitism.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:13.589
No matter when or where, and it ultimately
it boils down to the same tropes, the same

00:12:13.589 --> 00:12:15.589
prejudices, the same hatred.

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:17.779
Battling it is arduous.

00:12:17.779 --> 00:12:25.300
And yet: There’s so much more to being Jewish
– than fighting this hatred.

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:30.370
That's perhaps something I'd wish for: Continuing
to take a look at active Jewish life and showing

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:32.500
what's actually happening.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:36.560
Because there'’s always a great danger on
the Jewish side, too, that you only think

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:41.620
within these dimensions [of antisemitism]
and you actually forget the positive sides

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:42.620
a bit.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:45.690
Personally, I can't identify with this victim
role at all.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.374
I always wear my Star of David openly and
I am a proud Jew.

00:12:48.374 --> 00:12:51.790
And that's how I walk through the streets
of Munich, as a proud Jewish woman.

